Greetings,

Phase I - Business Understanding and Extraction
My PowerBI creation process first involves discussions with stakeholders to understand a base level of their requests and requirements.  
From there, I employ an Agile approach to rapidly build out an initial project.  During this initial phase, I gain an understanding of the data
using CRISP-DM (Cross-Industry Standard for Data Mining), meaning that I comb through the data and see how it fits and relates to the 
business needs and outcomes of the project.  After understanding the data, I then make the initial extractions and cleaning or sanitization.
Examples of cleaning and sanitization, fields that are mostly null may be removed, ensuring proper data types and fields lengths, and 
trimming unnecessary white space, etc.  All just basic data governance and hygiene.

Phase II - Minimum Viable Product and Transformation/Loading
Once I have selected the appropriate data, I fulfill the requests and requirements, and add
to those with many more reports, tables, and visuals.  Often times the client has a good idea of what they want, but does not always have a holistic
view of the options, so providing various additional perspective and context brings more insight and clarity to make better business decisions.  I do implement 
much of my own custom DAX, generally creating a single calendar table including "fiscal year" transformations, generate all of the basic aggregators and ratios,
and build a few non-connected tables to use as DAX-based filters in calculations.

Phase III - Technical Adjustments and Aesthetics
From there, based on stakeholder feedback I begin making adjustments to hone in on the exact nature of the requirements, any additional requests that
might have developed from seeing the MVP phase, technical calculations pertinent to the business or industry, and greatly improving the look and feel
of the report and dashboards.

Phase IV - Establishing Production Ready Workloads and Automations
Once the client has approved of the direction, and the project is about 70% or 80% ready for production, I will get the administrative end ready with
gateways, connections, and automations.  This will become the engineering piece to make sure that the SQL, Python, PySpark and other code is stable, has pipelines
built that automatically run through the ELT/ETL process.  Depending on resources available this will involve manual individualized connections, 
PowerAutomate and task schedulers for lower budgets, thrifty implementations of Azure Data Factory and Fabric for medium budgets, and as the resources
are expanded, implementing fully fledged stacks of ADF, Fabric, and Snowflake.

